    "Question_Text": "A diagnostic AI scanner detects a software vulnerability with 99% accuracy if present. If absent, it has a 2% false alarm rate. If only 0.5% of code modules actually contain this vulnerability, what is the probability that a module flagged as vulnerable by the scanner genuinely contains the vulnerability?",
    "Option_A": "20.0%",
    "Option_B": "19.9%",
    "Option_C": "33.2%",
    "Option_D": "15.4%",
    "Correct_Answer": "A",
    "Marks": 1,
    "Difficulty_Level": "DIFFICULT",
    "Explanation": "Let V = vulnerable, F = flagged. P(V) = 0.005, P(not V) = 0.995. P(F|V) = 0.99, P(F|not V) = 0.02. By Bayes Theorem: P(V|F) = [P(V)*P(F|V)] / [P(V)*P(F|V) + P(not V)*P(F|not V)] = [0.005 * 0.99] / [0.005*0.99 + 0.995*0.02] = 0.00495 / (0.00495 + 0.0199) = 0.00495 / 0.02485 ≈ 0.19919 (19.92% ≈ 20.0%).",
